course: biology
lesson: 328
title: Old pets: grey muzzles, slow walks and saying goodbye
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Pets grow old much faster than people. A dog that bounced around when you started school may have a grey muzzle, stiff legs and cloudy eyes by the time you finish primary school.
explain: Old pets need a different kind of care: shorter, gentler walks, soft beds, ramps and steps, hand signals for a deaf dog, furniture kept in place for a blind one, and regular vet checks of joints and teeth.
explain: Picture an old cat who no longer jumps onto her favourite high shelf. She does not cry or limp, because cats hide pain well, so that missing jump may be the only sign that her joints hurt.
explain: One day a very ill old pet may be suffering with no cure, and the vet and family may choose to put it to sleep gently and without pain. Feeling very sad afterwards is normal, because a pet is part of the family.
fact: Like human hair, a dog's fur loses its colour with age, and dogs count as older from about seven or eight, with big breeds ageing sooner.
fact: Many old dogs have stiff, sore joints called arthritis; shorter, gentler walks, soft beds, ramps and medicine from the vet help.
fact: Cats hide pain well, so not jumping onto high places is often the only sign of sore joints; in the wild a cat that showed pain could be attacked.
fact: An old dog's cloudy blue eyes often mean the lens has just hardened with age and the dog can still see, but sometimes it is a cataract that blocks sight, so the vet checks.
fact: Blind dogs manage well using their noses if the furniture stays in the same place.
fact: Many old dogs slowly lose their hearing, so hand signals and gentle touches work instead of shouting, and deaf dogs may get a fright if woken suddenly.
fact: One dog year does not equal seven human years: a dog's first year is more like 15 human years, then ageing slows, and small dogs usually live longer than big dogs.
fact: Among different kinds of animal, bigger ones usually live longer, so big dogs living shorter lives than small dogs is a puzzle scientists still study.
fact: A dog called Bluey in Australia was reported to live over 29 years, and a cat called Creme Puff in the USA was reported to live 38 years; these are rare records.
fact: Many old pets have tartar, a hard brown crust on the teeth, and sore gums, bad teeth can stop a pet eating, and a grown cat has 30 teeth, of which an old cat may have lost several.
fact: When an illness cannot be fixed and a pet is in pain, the vet can put it to sleep gently and without pain; the family decides together with the vet, and it is a kind choice.
fact: Missing a pet is normal; talking, drawing, a photo book or planting a tree can help, and other pets in the home may seem sad too.
fact: Older animals often wait the longest at rescue centres, though they are usually calm, trained and happy with a quiet life.
exercise: Why does an old dog's muzzle go grey? | Like human hair, its fur loses colour with age | It has been eating too much flour, and the dust sticks to its face | It is cold all the time | Grey fur grows only on sick dogs
exercise: From about what age do dogs count as older? | About seven or eight | From about one year old | About thirty years old | From about two months old
exercise: What often makes an old dog walk slowly and lag behind? | Stiff, sore joints called arthritis | It is too full of food every single day | Its fur has grown much too long and heavy | It has forgotten the way home from the park
exercise: How can you help an old dog with sore joints? | Shorter, gentler walks, soft beds and ramps | Longer and faster runs every day to make it fit again | A hard floor to sleep on | Jumping games every evening
exercise: Why might an old cat that stopped jumping onto a shelf be in pain? | Cats hide pain well, so a missed jump may be the only sign | Cats stop liking shelves at about age five, so it is nothing to worry about | Old cats are always lazy | The shelf has become too small
exercise: Why do cats hide pain? | In the wild a cat that showed pain could be attacked | They do not want their owners to take them to the vet's | Cats do not feel pain at all | They are proud of their fur
exercise: What do an old dog's cloudy blue eyes usually mean? | Often the lens has hardened with age, though a vet should check for cataract | The dog is always fully blind and can no longer see anything at all, even bright light | The dog is turning into a wolf | The dog has eaten something blue
exercise: How does a blind dog find its way around a home? | With its nose, if the furniture stays in place | By listening only to the sounds of the television and radio | With its tail, like a stick | It cannot move at all
exercise: How can you call an old deaf dog? | With hand signals and gentle touches | By shouting louder and louder until it hears you | By blowing a very high-pitched whistle | By ringing a loud bell right in its ear
exercise: What is wrong with «one dog year equals seven human years»? | A dog's first year is more like 15 human years, then ageing slows | Nothing, a dog ages exactly seven human years in each year of its life | Dogs age slower than people | A dog year is one human year
exercise: Which dogs usually live longer? | Small dogs | Big, heavy dogs | Dogs with long tails | Dogs with black fur
exercise: How old was the record dog Bluey reported to be? | Over 29 years | Over 90 years old | Exactly 7 years | About 12 years
exercise: How old was the record cat Creme Puff reported to be? | 38 years | Nearly 18 years | About 83 years | Only 8 years
exercise: Why might an old cat stop eating? | Sore teeth and gums can make eating hurt | Old cats prefer to eat only at night, when the house is quiet | Old cats do not need food any more at all | It is far too busy sleeping in the sun all day
exercise: What can a vet do for a very ill old pet that is suffering with no cure? | Put it to sleep gently and without pain, with the family | Give it a medicine that makes it young and healthy again | Send it to live on a farm | Nothing, the family must wait
exercise: What can help a child who is sad after a pet dies? | Talking, drawing, a photo book or planting a tree | Pretending the pet never lived | Never talking about it | Buying a new pet the next day so the sadness goes
exercise: Why might an older pet from a rescue centre suit a busy family? | It is usually calm, trained and happy with a quiet life | It can be left alone in the house for a whole week at a time | It never needs food | It does not need a vet
test: «gran says the dog's 3 so he's 21 in human years» What is closer to the truth? | A dog's first year is like about 15 human years, then ageing slows, so the sum is not that simple | Yes, gran is right: a dog ages exactly seven human years every single year, so the sum really is that simple | No, he is 3 in human years too | No, he is 70 in human years
test: Count them: an old cat had 30 teeth and the vet took out 4. How many are left? | 26 | 34 | 24 | 30
test: Count them: two old dogs and one old cat sleep by the fire. How many grey muzzles at most, legs and tails? | 3 muzzles, 12 legs and 3 tails | 3 muzzles, 12 legs and 6 tails | 2 muzzles, 8 legs and 3 tails | 3 muzzles, 6 legs and 3 tails
test: In a cartoon an old dog goes stiff, then the hero shouts and it jumps up young again. What really helps a stiff old dog? | Gentle walks, soft beds, ramps and medicine from the vet | Running it hard every day so its legs get strong again | Shouting at it loudly | Nothing can help an old dog
test: «dog's gone deaf, gonna sneak up and grab him for a laugh» Why not? | A deaf dog may get a fright and could snap; touch him gently where he can see | Old dogs never mind being grabbed, because they cannot feel much any more, so it is fine | Deaf dogs love surprises | He will hear you anyway
test: What would happen if the family moved all the furniture while their old dog was blind? | It would bump into things, because it remembers where things were | It would see again | Nothing, blind dogs fly over things | It would stop using its nose
test: «why do big dogs die younger, elephants live ages and they're huge» What is the best answer? | Among dogs the big ones usually age faster, a puzzle scientists still study | Big dogs are simply walked too much, and the exercise wears out their hearts | Big dogs actually live longest | Elephants are a kind of big dog
test: «vet said put the dog to sleep, that's cruel» What is the best reply? | When an illness cannot be fixed, ending the pain gently is a kind, loving choice | It only means the dog has a long, deep sleep for a week and then wakes up better | Yes, vets do it just to save time | Yes, the dog will wake up later
test: «cat's fine, she just won't jump on the shelf no more» What should the family think? | She may be hiding sore joints, so a vet check and a step would help | She has simply forgotten how to jump, which happens to all old cats | She is just being rude | She no longer likes the family
test: «my dog died and I can't stop crying, is that weird» What is the best answer? | No, missing a pet is normal because it was family | Yes, people should not cry about pets | Yes, you should forget it at once | Only small children cry about pets
test: Why does a deaf dog need hand signals instead of shouting? | It cannot hear words but can see the signals | Shouting makes its hearing come back | Dogs only understand shouting | Hand signals make it run faster
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the domestic cat conceals pain. | In the wild an animal displaying pain may be subject to attack | The cat is incapable of experiencing pain | The cat wishes to avoid veterinary attendance | The concealment is learnt from dogs
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the ageing of the domestic dog. | The first year corresponds to approximately fifteen human years, whereafter ageing proceeds more slowly | Each year of the dog's life corresponds to exactly seven human years, irrespective of the breed or size of the animal | The dog ages more slowly than the human being in every year | Larger breeds invariably live longer than smaller ones
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the decision to put an incurably ill and suffering animal to sleep? | It is a decision taken jointly by the family and the veterinarian to spare the animal pain | It is a punishment inflicted upon an animal for disobedience | It is a procedure from which the animal subsequently awakens | It is undertaken without regard to the animal's suffering
exam: Ascertain the assistance appropriate to an aged dog afflicted by arthritis. | Shorter and gentler walks, soft bedding, ramps and medication prescribed by a veterinarian | Prolonged and vigorous running on each day, together with jumping exercises to restore strength | The removal of all bedding | The provision of jumping exercises
